nio: lift PID1 priority before runq enqueue so it wins thread_select

690's full scheduler picture proved plain starvation: PID1 sits on CPU0's
own runq (p1rq=0), unbound (p1bd=f), at the default sched_pri=31 (p1pri=1f),
while 81/91-priority kernel threads are chosen every time.  choose_thread
is correct -- 31 simply cannot outrank the kernel band.

Raise PID1 to 94 inside thread_setrun(), after the priority recompute and
before sfi_thread_classify/runq_enqueue: the enqueue then places it in a
queue thread_select actually considers, with no post-hoc requeue (the 645
lesson: writing 96 panicked, writing 95 after enqueue had no effect).  94
is below BASEPRI_RTQUEUES(97) so it stays out of realtime_setrun, and above
every kernel-band priority.  Re-applied on each wakeup so QoS recalcs
cannot undo it.

diff --git a/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c b/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
index b426358..82f816b 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
@@ -5708,6 +5708,15 @@ thread_setrun(
 	if (SCHED(can_update_priority)(thread)) {
 		SCHED(update_priority)(thread);
 	}
+
+	/* nio: lift PID1 above the kernel-priority thread band just before
+	 * enqueue, so runq_enqueue places it where thread_select will pick it.
+	 * 94 < BASEPRI_RTQUEUES(97) avoids realtime_setrun, yet beats the
+	 * kernel threads (81/91) that were starving it.  Re-applied on every
+	 * wakeup via thread_setrun, so a QoS priority recalc cannot undo it. */
+	if (thread == nio_pid1_thread && thread->sched_pri < BASEPRI_RTQUEUES) {
+		thread->sched_pri = 94;
+	}
 	thread->sfi_class = sfi_thread_classify(thread);
 
 	if (thread->bound_processor == PROCESSOR_NULL) {
